      "A: **K‑Means Clustering – Quick Summary**\n",
      "\n",
      "- **Goal:** Partition data into *k* clusters; each point joins the cluster whose centroid (the mean of its points) is closest.\n",
      "- **Basic Algorithm**\n",
      "  1. Pick the number of clusters *k* and randomly set *k* initial centroids.\n",
      "  2. **Assignment step:** Assign every data point to the nearest centroid.\n",
      "  3. **Update step:** Re‑compute each centroid as the mean of the points assigned to it.\n",
      "  4. Repeat steps 2–3 until centroids no longer move (convergence) or a preset iteration limit is reached.\n",
      "\n",
      "- **Choosing *k* – The Elbow Method**\n",
      "  - Plot the within‑cluster sum of squares (WCSS) for various *k* values.\n",
      "  - Select the *k* at the “elbow” where the reduction in WCSS sharply slows.\n",
      "\n",
      "- **Limitations**\n",
      "  - Requires *k* to be set beforehand.\n",
      "  - Sensitive to initial centroid placement → may get stuck in local optima (mitigated by multiple runs or k‑means++ initialization).\n",
      "  - Assumes clusters are roughly spherical and similarly sized; performs poorly on irregular shapes.\n",
      "  - Outliers heavily influence centroids because they use the mean.\n",
      "\n"
     ]
    }
